-- APPLIED 2026-10-10 via scripts/run-sql-file.mjs. Not in schema_migrations.
-- 102: budget-room sentences rewritten to the measured budget tier (101: regional research + queue budget-room pass C1-C4, 2026-10-10). Places in C1-C4 were searched for cheaper printed tariffs (dharamshala, rest house) and none were found.


BEGIN;
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS backups.prose_cost_20261010 (tbl text, col text, key text, old_value text, saved_at timestamptz default now());
INSERT INTO backups.prose_cost_20261010 (tbl, col, key, old_value) VALUES
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chitrakoot/11', $cst$This is the comfortable month. Every site is accessible, the weather is pleasant all day (something no other month achieves), and the pilgrim crowds are manageable. The Kamadgiri parikrama at dawn — mist on the sacred hill, sunlight filtering through thinning canopy — is at its atmospheric best. Gupt Godavari cave temperatures are perfect. The Ram Ghat evening aarti has an intimacy that peak season loses. This is when first-time visitors should come if they want to understand Chitrakoot rather than just see it. Rooms: ₹400-1,000/night.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chitrakoot/12', $cst$December morning fog at Ram Ghat, lifting slowly to reveal the river and the temple-lined bank, is one of India's quietest spectacles. The aarti at dawn happens regardless of weather or attendance — three devotees or three hundred, the ritual continues. Kamadgiri parikrama in winter morning light, with breath visible and temple bells the only sound, strips the experience to its essence. Christmas and New Year are non-events here — Chitrakoot follows the Hindu calendar exclusively. Rooms: ₹300-800/night. December is for visitors who understand that emptiness and meaning are not opposites.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'guptkashi/7', $cst$The Kedarnath trek in July monsoon is an act of devotion, full stop. The trail is slippery, visibility drops to 50 metres in cloud, leeches attach to ankles through gaiters, and the river crossings near Rambara can be intimidating at high water. But thousands of pilgrims still walk it — the bhajan groups singing through rain, the shared struggle of the climb, the temple emerging from fog at 3583m — this is pilgrimage in its most ancient form. Helicopter services run 50-60% of scheduled flights in July. Ponies remain available but handlers charge premium rates (₹3500-5000) for monsoon risk. In Guptkashi, the Vishwanath Temple's monsoon rituals include special abhishekam ceremonies. The 2013 disaster memorial takes on added weight in monsoon — the rebuilt temple stands where the flood was most devastating. Rooms: ₹800-1500. Food stays reliable but limited to Indian basics. The SDRF maintains presence on the trail — look for their checkpoints for emergency contact.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'sissu/5', $cst$May Sissu is the first taste of Lahaul for most visitors. The Atal Tunnel exit is dramatic — you emerge from fluorescent-lit tunnel into a valley of completely different character from the Kullu side. The waterfall is in full roar — mist from its base drenches anyone standing at the roadside viewpoint. The village is small (maybe 200 houses) but photogenic: prayer flags, Buddhist gompa, stone-and-wood houses against dramatic mountain backdrop. The Gondhla Fort (8km toward Keylong) is a lesser-known gem — a wooden tower fort similar to Kinnauri architecture. The Tandi confluence (20km) where the Chandra and Bhaga rivers meet is the traditional gateway to Lahaul. Hotels at ₹1,000-2,500. May is the month when Sissu works as a destination, not just a tunnel exit stop.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'uttarkashi/12', $cst$December is Uttarkashi at its core: cold, clear, quiet, holy. The Kashi Vishwanath temple's winter aarti — a handful of devotees, the Bhagirathi audible in the background, mountain cold in the air — is Uttarkashi's essence. NIM winter courses train the next generation of Indian mountaineers on ice and snow. The town market sells winter provisions: rajma, rice, dried fruit, wool. Hotels at ₹400-800 with room heaters. The Bhagirathi in December — low, clear, blue — is the prettiest version of a river that will become the Ganga. December Uttarkashi strips away the Char Dham circus and shows you why this town has been sacred for centuries: it sits at the gates of the Himalayas, and in December, the gates are quiet.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'rudraprayag/4', $cst$April is Rudraprayag's last calm month. The town is preparing — hotels painting, restaurants expanding menus, jeep operators servicing vehicles. The pre-season energy is exciting if you know what's coming. The confluence is at ideal viewing: full from early snowmelt, clear enough to see the colour difference. Ukhimath (41km), where Kedarnath's deity spends winter, has temple ceremonies preparing for the return journey. This is insider access to the Char Dham machinery. Hotel rates start at ₹800-1,500 and climb through the month. The 2013 memorial site is reflective and important. Book May accommodation now if you're joining the pilgrimage season.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'sangla/9', $cst$Late September Sangla is a revelation. The monsoon parts like a curtain, revealing the Kinnaur Kailash range with fresh snow over green. The apple harvest fills the valley with activity, fragrance, and purpose. Temporary packing stations appear. Trucks line up on the road. The entire economy pivots to apples. Buying directly from an orchard — ₹60-100/kg for apples that retail at ₹200+ in Delhi — is one of India's great food experiences. Kamru Fort and Chitkul in September clarity are magnificent. The road stabilizes. Hotels at ₹800-1,800. The walk from Sangla to Kamru to Batseri is the valley's essential circuit. This is the insider's month.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'sissu/4', $cst$April's unfreeze is dramatic. The waterfall breaks free — watching it transition from ice column to roaring cascade over days is genuinely thrilling. The snow retreating up the mountainsides reveals terraced fields and walking paths. Sissu's wildflowers start early on sheltered south-facing slopes. The road to Keylong opens, and with it, the first glimpse of deeper Lahaul — Jispa, Darcha, and the Baralacha La approach. The village shakes off winter: guesthouses repaint, tea stalls reopen, the dhaba fires up. Hotel rates at ₹800-1,500 are pre-season affordable. The Atal Tunnel traffic increases — more cars with Manali plates exploring the accessible side of the tunnel. April Sissu is discovery travel: a village becoming accessible after six months of isolation (or in the modern era, accessible from Manali but isolated from the rest of Lahaul).$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'guptkashi/9', $cst$Late September combines the best of multiple seasons: monsoon clarity, reduced crowds, open trails, and comfortable temperatures. The Kedarnath trek post-monsoon is the insider's timing — the trail is clear, wildflowers still line the upper reaches, and you can actually see the peaks that pilgrims in May and July only imagine through cloud. Helicopter services run normally after mid-September. The temple stays open and darshan takes 15-30 minutes, no queue management barriers needed. Guptkashi's bazaar feels human-scaled again. The Kalimath Shakti Pith, 10 km away, hosts Navratri celebrations in late September with genuine village festival energy — not tourist-packaged, just community worship. Room rates: ₹1000-1800, easy availability. The Mandakini at post-monsoon levels is beautiful — full but not angry, clean and cold. This is genuinely the best month for combining Kedarnath pilgrimage with Guptkashi heritage in a single, calm trip.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'rudraprayag/11', $cst$November Rudraprayag is quiet, cold, and honest. The confluence in winter-approach light has a steel-blue beauty. Kedarnath has closed; the pilgrim traffic has ended. The town returns to its 5,000-person reality. The Koteshwar temple echoes with river sounds and little else. The surrounding peaks carry fresh snow. Evening comes early — by 5pm the valley fills with shadow and woodsmoke. Hotels at ₹600-1,000 are happy to see you. The walk along the Mandakini bank toward Augustmuni is cold but beautiful. This is Rudraprayag for people who care about confluences, not just destinations.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'rudraprayag/10', $cst$October is Rudraprayag's jewel month. The confluence sparkles in post-monsoon clarity. The Mandakini runs from blue to green depending on the light. The Alaknanda is deeper blue from higher-altitude sources. Together they create a visible meeting line that photographs beautifully. The surrounding terraced hills are post-monsoon lush. Kedarnath temple's closing ceremony (mid-October to early November) means the last pilgrim wave passes through. After that, the town exhales into genuine calm. The Koteshwar temple in October light is sublime. Hotels are reasonable (₹800-1,800). This is when you understand why Rudraprayag is one of the Panch Prayag — five sacred confluences.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_lead', 'guptkashi/11', $cst$Kedarnath is closed. Guptkashi returns to its village identity — the pilgrim infrastructure shuts down, guesthouses empty, and the Vishwanath Temple serves locals only. Temperatures drop to 2-10°C and the first frost appears reliably. The Mandakini valley in November is stark and lovely — bare trees, brown-gold grass, clear river. This is when Guptkashi reveals itself as a place, not just a base camp. The 12th-century Ardhnarishwar Temple, often overlooked in pilgrim season, deserves a slow visit. Rooms drop to ₹500-800.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_lead', 'guptkashi/8', $cst$August continues the monsoon siege. The Kedarnath trek remains open but conditions are genuinely challenging — 300-400mm of rain, regular landslide closures on the access road, and the trail above Gaurikund gets treacherously slick. Helicopter services operate weather-permitting, which in August means cancellations are common. Guptkashi itself stays functional — temples maintain daily schedules, shops stay open. The valley is impossibly green and waterfalls appear on every cliff face. Room rates drop to ₹800-1500 as tourist numbers thin. This is monsoon pilgrimage — you earn this darshan.$cst$);
